    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d513-a2">

                <pb facs="0489" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1900/pg391"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 2.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Wilcox to Mr. Squiers.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States Consulate</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Hankau
                                China</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1899-09-04"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">September 4, 1899</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 50.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to return herewith the
                    duplicate application of Logan H. Roots, and would kindly request that a
                    passport be granted him.</p>
                <p>In answer to a letter to the Department regarding missionaries who expected to
                    always remain in China, and who did not desire to sign the statement that they
                    intended to return to the United States to take up the duties of citizenship, I
                    received the following instructions:</p>
                <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Washington, January 23, 1899.</hi></p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. S. Wilcox</hi>, Esq.,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">United States Consul, Hankau</hi>.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The issuance of passports is not mandatory
                    upon the Secretary of State, but permissory. Recognizing that such of our
                    citizens who have gone to China to pursue their religious calling may not
                    return, but continue their work indefinitely abroad, the Department is disposed
                    to sanction their receiving passports on taking the oath of allegiance.</p>
                <p rendition="#right"><hi rendition="#small-caps">Thos. W. Cridler</hi>,<lb/><hi
                        rendition="#italic">Third Assistant Secretary.</hi></p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">L. S. Wilcox</hi>, <hi
                            rendition="#italic">Consul.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
